.Dd October 27, 2026
.Dt TOP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm top
.Nd display sorted information about processes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl a | Fl d | Fl e | Fl c Ar mode
.Op Fl F | Fl f
.Op Fl h
.Op Fl i Ar interval
.Op Fl l Ar samples
.Op Fl ncols Ar columns
.Op Fl o Ar key | Fl O Ar skey
.Op Fl R | Fl r
.Op Fl S
.Op Fl s Ar delay-secs
.Op Fl n Ar nprocs
.Op Fl stats Ar keys
.Op Fl pid Ar processid
.Op Fl user Ar username
.Op Fl U Ar username
.Op Fl u
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility periodically displays a summary of the system and a sorted list
of processes.
The summary gives the number of processes and threads, the load
averages, the CPU usage since the previous sample, the physical memory
in use, the virtual memory, the swap (with
.Fl S )
and the packets and bytes the network interfaces moved.
.Pp
Interactively,
.Nm
redraws the screen every
.Ar delay-secs
seconds and shows as many processes and columns as fit.
When its output is not a terminal, or with
.Fl l ,
it prints its samples one after another instead.
The first sample in that logging mode has no CPU percentages, as there
is nothing yet to compare with.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "-c mode"
.It Fl a
Count the event columns since
.Nm
started
.Pq Fl c Cm a .
.It Fl c Ar mode
Set how the event columns
.Pq Cm faults , cow , msgsent , msgrecv , sysbsd , sysmach , csw , pageins
are counted:
.Cm a
since
.Nm
started,
.Cm d
since the previous sample,
.Cm e
or
.Cm n
(the default) since each process started.
.It Fl d
Count the event columns since the previous sample
.Pq Fl c Cm d .
.It Fl e
Count the event columns since each process started
.Pq Fl c Cm e .
.It Fl F , Fl f
Accepted for compatibility; shared library information is not shown.
.It Fl h
Print the usage and exit.
.It Fl i Ar interval
Accepted for compatibility.
.It Fl l Ar samples
Logging mode: print
.Ar samples
samples, or with 0, until interrupted.
.It Fl n Ar nprocs
Show at most
.Ar nprocs
processes.
.It Fl ncols Ar columns
In logging mode, show only the first
.Ar columns
columns.
.It Fl O Ar skey
Sort by
.Ar skey
when the primary key ties.
.It Fl o Ar key
Sort by
.Ar key
.Pq default Cm cpu .
A
.Ql +
or
.Ql -
before the key sorts in ascending or descending order; without one,
text keys sort ascending and numbers descending.
.It Fl pid Ar processid
Show only this process; may be given more than once.
.It Fl R , Fl r
Accepted for compatibility; the memory object map is not traversed.
.It Fl S
Show the swap usage in the summary.
.It Fl s Ar delay-secs
Wait
.Ar delay-secs
seconds between samples (default 1).
.It Fl stats Ar keys
Show the columns of these keys, in this order, separated by commas.
.It Fl U Ar username , Fl user Ar username
Show only the processes of this user (a name or a user ID).
.It Fl u
Sort by CPU usage, then by time
.Pq Fl o Cm cpu Fl O Cm time .
.El
.Sh KEYS
.Bl -tag -width "msgrecv" -compact
.It Cm pid
process ID
.It Cm command
command name
.It Cm cpu
CPU usage since the previous sample, in percent
.It Cm time
CPU time, user and system
.It Cm threads
number of threads
.Pq alias Cm th
.It Cm wq
number of workqueue threads
.It Cm mem
resident memory
.It Cm vsize
virtual size
.It Cm pgrp
process group
.It Cm ppid
parent process ID
.It Cm state
running, sleeping, stopped or zombie
.It Cm uid
user ID
.It Cm user
user name
.It Cm faults
page faults
.It Cm cow
copy-on-write faults
.It Cm msgsent
Mach messages sent
.It Cm msgrecv
Mach messages received
.It Cm sysbsd
BSD system calls
.It Cm sysmach
Mach system calls
.It Cm csw
context switches
.It Cm pageins
pageins
.El
.Pp
These keys are not available yet and are an error:
.Cm boosts , cmprs , cpu_me , cpu_others , cycles , idlew , instrs ,
.Cm kprvt , kshrd , mregion , ports , power , purg , rprvt , vprvt .
.Sh INTERACTION
While
.Nm
redraws the screen, these keys are read:
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It Ic \&?
show a help screen
.It Ic q
quit
.It Ic o
set the primary sort key
.It Ic O
set the secondary sort key
.It Ic s
set the delay between samples
.It Ic n
set the number of processes shown
.It Ic U
show only one user's processes (an empty answer shows all)
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Usage errors exit with 64.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Print two samples of the five processes using the most CPU:
.Pp
.Dl $ top -l 2 -n 5 -stats pid,command,cpu,mem
.Pp
Watch one process's context switches, per sample:
.Pp
.Dl $ top -pid 1 -c d -stats pid,command,csw
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ps 1 ,
.Xr uptime 1
.Sh BUGS
Another user's processes show no CPU time, memory or counters unless
.Nm
runs as the super-user.
